+<section class="blurb">
+<p><a href="http://cds.cern.ch/record/1165534/files/CERN-Brochure-2009-003-Eng.pdf"><img alt="Technicians get around the tunnel on bicycles" src="/sites/home.web.cern.ch/files/image/inline-images/old/tunnel-transport.jpg" /></a></p>
+<h1><a href="http://cds.cern.ch/record/1165534/files/CERN-Brochure-2009-003-Eng.pdf">Facts and Figures [PDF]</a></h1>
+<p>How many kilometres of cables are there on the LHC? How lowis the pressure in the beam pipe? Discover facts and figures about the in the handy LHC guide</p>
+<h3><a href="http://cds.cern.ch/record/1165534/files/CERN-Brochure-2009-003-Eng.pdf">Download the LHC guide [PDF]</a></h3>
+</section>
+
+<section class="blurb">
+<p><a href="http://press.web.cern.ch/backgrounders/safety-lhc"><img alt="CERN firefighters during their daily safety training" event"="" src="/sites/home.web.cern.ch/files/image/inline-images/old/fire_safety.jpg" /></a></p>
+<h1><a href="http://press.web.cern.ch/backgrounders/safety-lhc">Safety of the LHC</a></h1>
+<p>CERN takes safety very seriously. Thisreport by the LHC Safety Assessment Group (LSAG) confirms that LHC collisions present no danger and that there are no reasons for concern</p>
+<h3><a href="http://press.web.cern.ch/backgrounders/safety-lhc">Read about the safety of the LHC</a></h3>
+</section>
